k": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}], "id": 10}, {"stimuli_ids": [], "template_override": 1, "questions": [{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "enpe8n7le03ob09re7pdf01.pdf"}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "selected": 0, "correct": 1, "id": 0}], "id": 0}], "type": "DWS", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Tips on IT Procurement"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Take Away"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}, {"type": "text", "content": ""}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n7le03ob09re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n7le03ob09re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Deliverables in an IT Procurement</strong><br/><br/>\nEven though it may sound simple to the average customer, some supplier contracts are purposely imprecise on what should be delivered. The truth isn't as clear-cut as one might expect. Reliable supplier software licenses don't usually contain a warranty that the software will work in accordance with specifications and don't provide any functional warranties whatsoever. In the same way, supplier services agreements are usually more based on a consulting arrangement than on outcomes."}, {"time": "33300", "image": "enpe8n7le03ob09re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "Since software is the usual deliverable in an IT procurement, its functionality should be clearly set out in a schedule within the contract. Services being provided should be described just as clearly as the functionality since any kind of failure in this sense will result in everything becoming \u201cadditional service\u201d for additional fees. If it is the case of ongoing services, particular service levels ought to be included for tangible outputs of the service. You may also want to consider as deliverable a service credit or rebate concept both to drive supplier commitment and to obtain easier access to compensation if services are not provided in conformity with the agreement."}, {"time": "75000", "image": "enpe8n7le03ob09re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "Most IT projects take time to be undertaken and suppliers do not like being responsible for timeframes. It is extremely important that your agreement includes a clear project plan with elemental main points for the supplier to meet, and having a schedule is a good option to avoid difficulties. Liquidated damages being payable may also be taken into account in case the milestones in the contract are not met. <br/>\nIn order to prevent negative outcomes when a deliverable does not meet the expected specifications, it is highly recommended to obtain a right to perform acceptance testing of the solution and obtain a compensation."}], "coverimg": "enpe8n7le03ob09re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "type": "scholar", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0}]}